WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.654
In this video we cover how a Bluetooth Low Energy device exposes its data through GATT.

2
00:00:06.854 --> 00:00:13.392
GATT stands for Generic Attribute Profile, and it sits on top of the Attribute Protocol, called ATT.

3
00:00:13.592 --> 00:00:19.746
Before any GATT traffic happens, the devices find each other using GAP, the Generic Access Profile.

4
00:00:19.946 --> 00:00:23.792
A peripheral advertises on three channels: thirty-seven, thirty-eight, and thirty-nine.

5
00:00:23.992 --> 00:00:30.530
The central scans those channels and sends a connection request when it finds the device it wants.

6
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:37.653
Once connected, the peripheral usually acts as the GATT server and the phone acts as the GATT client.

7
00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:42.853
Data is organized into services, and each service contains one or more characteristics.

8
00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:48.053
For example, the standard heart rate service has the sixteen bit UUID 0x180D.

9
00:00:48.253 --> 00:00:54.407
Inside it, the heart rate measurement characteristic is what actually carries the beats per minute value.

10
00:00:54.607 --> 00:00:58.838
Every characteristic has properties such as read, write, notify, and indicate.

11
00:00:59.038 --> 00:01:05.576
Notify lets the server push a new value without the client asking, and there is no acknowledgement.

12
00:01:05.776 --> 00:01:13.084
Indicate does the same thing but the client must confirm each value, so it is slower and more reliable.

13
00:01:13.284 --> 00:01:20.976
To turn notifications on, the client writes the value one to the Client Characteristic Configuration Descriptor, often shortened to CCCD.

14
00:01:21.176 --> 00:01:23.099
Writing two instead enables indications.

15
00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:29.453
A common bug is forgetting that CCCD write, and then wondering why no data ever arrives.

16
00:01:29.653 --> 00:01:35.422
The default ATT MTU is twenty-three bytes, which leaves twenty bytes of payload per notification.

17
00:01:35.622 --> 00:01:41.776
Most modern phones negotiate a larger MTU right after connecting, often two hundred and forty-seven bytes.

18
00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:47.745
If you need more throughput, also request the two megabit PHY, which Bluetooth 5 introduced.

19
00:01:47.945 --> 00:01:53.330
Connection interval matters too: a shorter interval means lower latency but higher power draw.

20
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:59.684
iOS will not let you go below fifteen milliseconds for the connection interval in most cases.

21
00:01:59.884 --> 00:02:05.653
For debugging, I use nRF Connect on the phone to browse services and toggle notifications.

22
00:02:05.853 --> 00:02:13.161
To see what is happening over the air, a sniffer running on an nRF52840 dongle feeds packets into Wireshark.

23
00:02:13.361 --> 00:02:21.053
The sniffer has to follow the connection from the very first connection request, otherwise it cannot track the channel hopping.

24
00:02:21.253 --> 00:02:24.715
So start the capture before you connect, not after.

25
00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:31.069
That is the core of GATT: services, characteristics, descriptors, and the CCCD switch that starts notifications.
